<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="matairesinol biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5466" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Lignans are a group of dimeric and/or oligomeric plant natural compounds of phenylpropanoid origin. They represent a structurally highly diverse compound class that is characterized by a 8,8&apos;-linkage found in the C6-C3 dimers of coniferyl alcohol or other cinnamoyl alcohols although other interunit linkages (e.g. 8-5&apos;, 8-O-4) may also occur |CITS: [Lewis99]| |CITS: [FUSS03]|. Lignans and lignins are biosynthesized in two major branches which descend from the phenylpropanoid pathway. During the conquest of land this pathway has been substantially advanced with consequences for derivative biosyntheses such as for lignins/lignans, hydrolyzable and condensed tannins and flavonoids |CITS: [STAFFORD00]|. Lignans are widespread among the plant kingdom but have not been found in aquatic plants such as algae indicating their crucial contribution to adaptation and fitness of terrestrial plants |CITS: [AYRES90][11423139]|. The structural biopolymers lignins and lignans accomplish different tasks in plants, lignins are involved in structural support and lignans have been attributed to protection and defense as their primary physiological functions. Taken together lignins and lignans count for about 30% of carbon in vascular plants |CITS: [Lewis99]|. Beside their recognized functions as defense compounds in plants some lignans have attracted the attention of scientists because of their application in medicine and human health |CITS: [MACRAE84]|. Lignans possess most effective health protective and curative properties and have been employed as antioxidants |CITS: [14995053]|, anti-carcinogenic agents |CITS: [10800470]| |CITS: [16311631]| and phytoestrogens |CITS: [15377220]|. Lignans can be subdivided into several structural subfamilies. The matairesinol pathway is an excellent example for a pathway that not only contains several types of lignans but also gives rise to other lignan subfamilies. (+)-pinoresinol (a furofuran) is sequentially converted to (+)-lariciresinol (a benzylaryltetrahydrofuran), (-)-secoisolariciresinol (a dibenzylbutane) and (-)-matairesinol (a dibenzylbutyrolactone) which is an intermediate in the biosynthetic route towards podophyllotoxin (an aryltetrahydronaphthalene) (compare |FRAME:PWY-5479|). &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The pathway begins with the formation of |FRAME: CPD-18761| from |FRAME: CONIFERYL-ALCOHOL|. Whether |FRAME: EC-1.11.1.7|, or |FRAME: EC-1.10.3.2|, function in the oxidation of monolignols to radicals has been under active evaluation for many years |CITS: [13657039][17812228][Sterjiades93][9914531]|. Both peroxidases and laccases were shown to catalyze the oxidation of |FRAME: CONIFERYL-ALCOHOL| to a radical &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [16027971][25626739]|. The next step involves the stereo-specific coupling of two phenoxy radicals to form dimers. Most lignans are optically active as a result of specific regio- and stereo specificity in the coupling process. Interestingly, the proteins responsible for the linkages are not enzymes. These proteins, which were named &apos;dirigent proteins&apos; (DP) serve only to bind and orientate the coniferyl alcohol-derived free radicals |CITS: [10859176][16023845][8994027]|. It could be demonstrated that dirigent proteins form a multigene family in plants |CITS: [12359517]|, and their function is not limited to the formation of lignans but also includes other biosyntheses of various phenolics employing corresponding radicals. The next step in the pathway is catalyzed by two distinct enzyme classes of pinoresinol/lariciresinol reductases (PLR). These bifunctional enzymes are stereospecific and catalyze the enantioselective reduction of either (+)-pinoresinol and (+)-lariciresinol towards (-)-secoisolaricinol (as discovered in Forsythia) |CITS: [8910615]| or (-)-pinoresinol and (-)-lariciresinol, forming (+)-secoisolaricinol, as demonstrated in western red cedar |CITS: [9872995]|. Similar enantiospecific enzymes have also been found in different species of the same genus |CITS: [15949826]|. Another bifunctional enzyme, |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.331| (SDH) affords the formation of |FRAME: CPD-8912| |CITS: [2037574]| via a stable lactol intermediate |CITS: [11278426]|.</Description>
